 the enzyme  immunoassay and the multiplex systems  definitely have the best specificity of  all the methods but again they don't  achieve quite the highest sensitivity of  the IFA and so do we think the clinical  testing for AAS is going to converge to  a single methodology and I actually  believe this is very unlikely in the  near future and I think labs will  continue to have to deal with these  different methodologies their analytical  differences as well as their differences  in clinical performance so that ends my  portion of the talk and I just put in  the reference for the study that I had  mentioned it has been published in the  journal Applied Lab medicine and if  you're interested in more detail I  encourage you to check out that  manuscript so I thank you all for your  attention  thank you so much doctor Abel of dr.  Schneider for your presentation a quick  reminder to the audience on how to  submit questions simply type them into  the Q&amp;A box found by clicking on the  green Q&amp;A button on the lower left of  the presentation window before we get  started in that Q&amp;A session we have one  final poll for you  did this webinar provide you with  helpful information that you can use in  your choice of an A and a screening  method we would appreciate your  participation so let's get started with  our audience Q&amp;A  dialogue dr. evil the first question is  for you you described a problem with  reproducibility for a n/a IFA do a na e  ia and multiplex show the same  variability issues is that an AI effect  medically if it is therefore manually  with a human element and subjectivity of  the evaluation of the Tigers eat it is  naturally has a typo usability issue  however with the EIA or the multiple  access say we don't really see this  problem because there definitely is much  greater the with the multiplex it's  about plus minus 10% with the AI even  better between 5% the reproducibility so  the revenues basically nothing should be  dissolved committed methods they are a  lot more like clinical chemistry then  floor then manual microscopy so we don't  see this issue with automated methods  thank you so much dr. Abel this next  question is for you dr. Schneider the  ROC curve comparing a nai FA e ia and  multiplex shows similar performance  can you speak to why you think there  still remains a strong focus by many  laboratories on IFA as the right method  of screening sure so I think it's a  number of reasons I think we touched on  those a little bit in the presentation  today many rheumatologists are still  view the a a bi IFA as the as the best  methodology to use so I think that  sometimes laboratories feel the the  pressure from their clinicians and  rheumatologists to maintain that  methodology despite the fact that there  is you know more and more data out there  showing that from a clinical utility  perspective the multiplex and the enzyme  immunoassay actually have comparable  characteristics if not if not even  improved and in some areas so I do think  that is an issue there is still the  issue related to proficiency testing and  and laboratories you know if they do run  an enzyme immunoassay or a multiplex  assay and they and there's a sample in  the survey that includes one of these  more rare antigen specificities that is  not detected by the enzyme immunoassay  or the multiplex assay then the  laboratory needs to deal with you know  how to handle that as far as you know  not not detecting that and and possibly  failing that survey so that's another  another issue as well and I and I do  think that a C our position statement  definitely you know carried a lot of  weight and many laboratories have been  hesitant to go to go against the  recommendation of the American College  of Rheumatology so so I think those  aren't just a few of the reasons why  many labs have elected to stay with the  IFA method despite you many  and the limitations that we've talked  about today thank you so much dr. Abel  this next question is for you in your  opinion what is the best automated IFA  platform as we do not have an automated  IFA platform as I as I mentioned we  don't offer the IFA anymore in houses to  send out so I really cannot  unfortunately I cannot comment on it  wonderful our next question dr. Abel is  for you do the automatic automated IFA  readers address the challenges with  specificity for a and a IFA tighter  speckled patterns that's all right  that's what we had the specificity issue  was related to low titer speckled  patterns so that was fine Schneider  pointed out that the specificity is  really decided by the cutoff that's used  so it's it's not as much they related to  the automated or manual reading the  automated manual ringing that makes a  big difference with the automated  reading is much more consistent than the  manual reading by different individuals  or even by the same individual at  different times of the same specimen the  automated readers issue that yes that  issue however the specificities really  determined a lot more by the cutoff  that's used for I favor for all the  other methods so I feel it itself  doesn't it doesn't address the  specificity it  it still you know if you you you it's  except like low titer low titer  special patterns is positive then the  specific thing will be very low  thank you dr. Abel dr. Schneider this  last question is for you and again thank  you audience for your participation dr.  Schneider your laboratory offers  multiple methods for a na testing does  your lab will run the CAC survey on both  methods and the second part of that  question is how do you reconcile  divergent results thanks that's a that's  a really great question because many  labs struggle with this issue not just  for AMA testing but if they if they test  for a single analyte using multiple  methods and actually cap requires on  their proficiency testing surveys that  if you're performing one analyte on  different methodologies that you only  can use their survey for one of those  methods whatever your primary method is  so the way we handle for AMA our cat  proficiency testing is that we report to  cap our four four proficiency testing  our AMA results from the enzyme  immunoassay and then what we do after  the survey has closed we use the  remaining sample that we have to test by  the IFA methodology and then use those  results to compare to our own method  comparison as cap would do as well as  with our peer group via the IFA or the  EIA now the second part of the question  is a little more challenging how do we  reconcile those discordant results  because we know that it will happen on  occasion specifically related to the to  the methodology to the particular  antibody that's within the patient  within the cap sample we in large part  do rely on the study that we we  performed recent the one I showed you  today that data to just basically  demonstrate that even though we do see  discordant  samples that the clinical utility of our  of our AIA testing or our IFA testing we  certainly look at the titer of the  antibody and how strongly positive it is  but in addition to that for our AMA by  IFA we actually also participate in  several other proficiency testing  surveys not just with cap and so we feel  that by by challenging ourselves more  with different surveys where we're able  to kind of understand more about how our  methods are performing on a routine  basis thank you again dr. Abel dr.  Schneider for your wonderful  presentation I'd also like to thank lab  reach and our sponsor bio-rad for making  today's educational webcast possible  before we go I want to let everyone know  that today's a webcast will be available  for on-demand viewing through April 2018  while rod will be replying to all  further questions from our audience  members you'll receive an email from lab  roots letting you know when this webcast  will be available for replay please  share this announcement with your  colleagues who may have missed today's  live event that's all for now and thanks  for joining us we hope to see you again  soon goodbye
 

